    "snippet": "---\ntitle: The Olympic Games in art\nauthor: Maggie Hills\ndate: 2025-01-01\n---\nAs the 2024 Summer Olympics and Paralympics approach, we thought we'd showcase some Olympic-themed art. From the history of the Games and portraits of sporting heroes to artworks capturing athletes in action ... ready, steady go!\n\nSporting shapes\n\nUsing a simple composition of shapes, bold colours and outlined figures, artist John Dugger has created a punchy, iconic sporting image. The gymnastic rings suggest the rings of the Olympics. Try creating your own image of an Olympic or Paralympic sport. Which sport will you choose and how can you use shapes and colours to simply and powerfully represent it?\n\nJohn Dugger (1948\u20132023)\n\nAcrylic and drawing on canvas and wood\n\nH 122 x W 200.5 cm\n\nTate\n\nOlympic countdown clocks\n\nTime is ticking \u2026 the Paris Olympic Countdown clock, located near the iconic Eiffel Tower, has been counting down the seconds, minutes and hours until the Opening Ceremony on 26 July 2024. The sculpture in this photograph incorporates the countdown clock for the London 2012 Olympics. The electronic countdown screens have served their purpose and are now blank, but the sculpture of a planet with a band of commemoration text has become a permanent reminder of the historic event.\n\nTake two: London Olympic Games\n\nMany of us may remember the 2012 London Olympics, but did you know London also hosted the Games in 2008 and 2012? Students at St Joseph's College in Upper Norwood Croydon, created these mosaics in 2010 to celebrate the 1948 and 2012 Olympics. The 1948 mural was based on an official poster designed for that event.\n\nCompare the places and buildings in London featured in each mural \u2026 which events are highlighted in the 2012 mural and what does this tell us about how the Olympics have developed?\n\nSebastian Coe, athlete and driving force behind the London 2012 bid\n\nRunner Sebastian Coe was born in London and brought up in Sheffield. He started training and competing with the Hallamshire Harriers athletics club in Sheffield when he was 12. In 1980 he held four world records simultaneously and went on to win a gold medal in the 1500m at the Olympics that year. At the 1984 Olympics he set an Olympic record for the 1500m. Coe was"
